  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nOne more day of lighter winds, above average warmth and a cool\nnight is coming up for Saturday as the surface reflection of\nIsaias passes to our east. As we head into early next week, the \npattern begins to change. An upper trough moves into the western \nCONUS, with downstream ridging aloft over the Plains. At the \nsurface, lee side low pressure will expand and strengthen, and \nsouth winds will ramp up as a result. The stronger winds, above \naverage warm temps and continued dry weather will yield increasing\nfire weather concerns, especially across northeast Oklahoma and \nfar northwest Arkansas Sunday and Monday. \n\nBy Tuesday, models bring a shallow cold front south across KS \nand MO and stall the boundary close to the state lines Tuesday \nnight before retreating north. Other solutions have this front\ngradually sagging south ahead of the southwest CONUS upper trough,\nwith expanding storm coverage over the northern forecast area. \n\nThe new 12Z GFS has come more in line with the faster 00Z EC \nregarding timing of the ejecting southwest CONUS upper trough into\nthe Plains. This also agreed with the bulk of the multi-model \nensemble solutions. As such, the timing appears favorable for \nshowers and storms to develop to our west Wednesday and sweep east\ninto our area by Wednesday night. Some of these storms could be \nsevere. Storms will probably linger over southern and eastern \nareas Thursday along the trailing front before the front clears \nthe area to the south and east by the end of the week.\n\nLacy",
